.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt IXLNVM 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ixlnvm
.Nd manage the NVM of Intel 700-series Ethernet controllers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm dump
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm show
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm cfg-read Ar feature_id
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm selftest
.Op Fl y
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm image-diff Ar file
.Nm ixlnvm
.Cm image-show Ar file
.Nm ixlnvm
.Op Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm match
.Fl c Ar nvmupdate.cfg
.Op Fl e Ar eepid
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm verify-checksum
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm image-apply
.Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl y
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm module-update
.Fl m Ar module
.Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl y
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads and updates the non-volatile memory
.Pq Dq NVM ,
also referred to as Flash, of an Intel 700-series Ethernet controller
attached via the
.Xr ixl 4
driver.
It uses the kernel's NVM session ioctls to hold ownership of the
NVM resource across multiple admin-queue commands.
.Pp
The interface to operate on is selected with
.Fl i Ar ifname .
.Pp
The available commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm dump Op Fl o Ar file
Read the entire 64 KB NVM as raw bytes.
With
.Fl o Ar file ,
the contents are written to
.Ar file ;
otherwise to standard output.
Writing the binary blob to a terminal is refused; redirect or use
.Fl o .
.It Cm show
Read the NVM header and print a summary of the well-known module
pointers and the software checksum word.
.It Cm cfg-read Ar feature_id
Issue an NVM Config Read admin command (datasheet 3.4.10.4) for the
given 16-bit
.Ar feature_id
and print the 6-byte response.
For example,
.Ar feature_id
0xFFF1 reads the current link-mode selection (1x40G, 2x40G, or 4x10G;
see datasheet 3.4.5.7).
This command waits for the asynchronous completion event posted on
the chip's admin receive queue.
.It Cm selftest Op Fl y
Read NVM word 0x16 (the PBA Block pointer, an RW header word per
datasheet 3.4.5.3) and write the same value back via NVM_UPDATE with
the Last_Command bit set, exercising the asynchronous bank-swap and
the kernel's ARQ completion-event demultiplex.
The card performs a bank swap on Flash, but the NVM contents are
semantically unchanged (identical-value write keeps the software
checksum valid).
Read back and compare to confirm.
.Pp
Without
.Fl y ,
prints what would happen and exits.
Pass
.Fl y
to confirm and run the test.
.It Cm image-show Ar file
Parse an Intel NVM update image
.Pq Ar file
and print its NVM control word, software checksum, PBA number,
and the table of module pointers (with the update flow each module
needs).
This subcommand operates on a file and does not require
.Fl i .
.It Cm image-diff Ar file
Verify the chip against an Intel NVM image
.Pq Ar file ,
module by module.
For each NVM module the tool understands, the module is located via
the
.Em chip's
own header pointer
.Pq not the image's, since a per-module update relocates modules and swaps banks ,
its bytes are read from the chip, and they are compared to the image's
copy of that module.
Each module is reported as one of:
.Bl -tag -width PRESERVED -compact
.It Cm MATCH
chip content equals the image.
.It Cm DIFFER
chip content differs; for a module the image carries, this means the
module is not yet updated on the chip.
.It Cm MANAGED
a chip-populated register auto-load or data-recovery module
.Pq e.g. Cm GLOBR Auto-load , Cm SW-Data-Recovery :
it differs from the static image by design
.Pq the firmware fills it with computed/runtime state
and is excluded from the pass/fail tally.
.It Cm PRESERVED
a card-identity field
.Pq MAC, VPD, PBA, PXE, VLAN, boot config
that an apply deliberately leaves untouched.
.It Cm ABSENT No / Cm SKIP
the module is not present in the image, or its size cannot be
determined.
.El
.Pp
A correctly updated card reports every authenticated and
non-authenticated module as
.Cm MATCH
and zero
.Cm DIFFER ;
the
.Cm MANAGED
modules differ identically to how Intel's own tool leaves them.
This is the recommended way to confirm an
.Cm image-apply
took effect.
.It Xo
.Cm match
.Fl c Ar nvmupdate.cfg
.Op Fl e Ar eepid
.Xc
Parse Intel's
.Ar nvmupdate.cfg
manifest and identify which packaged
.Pa .bin
image is appropriate for a given EETRACK ID.
Without
.Fl e ,
.Fl i Ar ifname
must be given and the chip's current EETRACK ID is read from words
0x2d-0x2e of its NVM.
With
.Fl e ,
the EETRACK ID is taken from the command line as a hex string.
.Pp
The result names the matching
.Cm NVM IMAGE
file, its target EEPID, the required reset type
.Pq POWER No or Cm REBOOT ,
and whether the chip is already at the matched image or needs to
upgrade to it (via the cfg's
.Cm REPLACES
list).
.It Cm verify-checksum
Read the chip's shadow RAM and verify the software checksum at
word 0x3f against the value an independent computation derives
from the same bytes (datasheet 6.1.4.1).
A match confirms that the chip's NVM state is internally consistent
and that this tool's checksum algorithm agrees with the firmware's.
.It Xo
.Cm image-apply
.Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl y
.Xc
Apply an Intel NVM image to the chip by running the per-module update
flow for each module in turn, in three phases:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Phase 1: the non-authenticated outside-shadow modules
.Pq datasheet 3.4.5.4 :
.Cm EMP Global ,
.Cm Manageability ,
.Cm EMP Settings ,
.Cm PHY Config Scripts ,
.Cm Configuration Metadata ,
and
.Cm External 25G PHY .
.It
Phase 2 (only with
.Fl s ,
.Sy experimental ) :
the read/write shadow-RAM modules
.Pq datasheet 3.4.5.3 .
This is not part of the supported upgrade: the chip's EMP regenerates
most of these modules on its own, and direct shadow writes have proven
fragile and chip-state-dependent.
See
.Sx CAVEATS .
.It
Phase 2b: the NVM header version words
.Pq Cm DEV_STARTER_VERSION No word 0x18, Cm EETRACK ID No words 0x2d-0x2e
and the recomputed software checksum, so the chip reports the new
image version after the upgrade.
.It
Phase 3: the authenticated outside-shadow modules
.Pq datasheet 3.4.5.5 :
.Cm PCIe Analog ,
.Cm PHY Analog ,
.Cm Option ROM ,
and
.Cm EMP Image .
.El
.Pp
.Cm EMP Image
is applied last: after its
.Cm LAST_COMMAND
the chip stops servicing admin-queue commands until power-cycle/reboot,
so it must be the final step.
Each authenticated module's RSA-2048 signature is verified by the chip
on
.Cm LAST_COMMAND
and a mismatch causes the chip to reject that module
.Pq the previous one stays active .
Modules absent from the image
.Pq pointer 0xffff/0x0000
are skipped.
.Pp
Without
.Fl y ,
.Cm image-apply
is a dry run: validates the image checksum, EETRACK ID, and per-module
source offsets, and prints the per-module plan.
With
.Fl y ,
the chip is actually written.
A reboot is required afterwards.
.It Xo
.Cm module-update
.Fl m Ar module
.Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl y
.Xc
Update one of the authenticated outside-shadow modules from an
Intel NVM image using the per-module update flow (datasheet
3.4.5.5).
The module is identified by name with
.Fl m :
.Cm option-rom ,
.Cm emp-image ,
.Cm pcie-analog ,
or
.Cm phy-analog .
The chip verifies the module's RSA-2048 signature before swapping
pointers; if verification fails, the chip rejects the update and
the old module remains active.
.Pp
The module area
.Pq 1160 KB for Cm option-rom No and Cm emp-image , 8 KB for the Cm Pq Analog No modules
is written sector by sector with interleaved
.Cm NVM_Erase
and
.Cm NVM_Update
admin-queue commands, the final command carrying
.Cm LAST_COMMAND .
.Pp
Without
.Fl y ,
prints the plan and exits.
With
.Fl y ,
the chip is actually written.
A reboot is required afterwards.
.El
.Sh SECURITY
Reading the NVM
.Pq Cm dump , show , scan , image-diff , verify-checksum , No etc.
is permitted at any
.Xr securelevel 7 .
Writing the NVM
.Pq Cm image-apply , module-update No with Fl y
opens a write session, which the kernel allows only at securelevel 0
or below.
In the default multi-user securelevel
.Pq 1 ,
with the network up, opening a write session fails with
.Er EPERM .
This prevents a privileged remote attacker from flashing malicious
firmware on a running, network-reachable system: to update firmware,
boot into single-user mode
.Pq which is administered from the local console and starts no network services ,
apply the image, and reboot.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std ixlnvm
.Sh EXAMPLES
Dump the NVM of
.Li ixl0
to a file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ixlnvm -i ixl0 dump -o ixl0.nvm
.Ed
.Pp
Display the NVM header summary:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ixlnvm -i ixl0 show
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ixl 4 ,
.Xr ifconfig 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Ox 7.9 .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Stefan Huber Aq Mt stefan.huber@stusta.de
.Sh CAVEATS
While an
.Nm
session is open on an interface, concurrent use of
.Ic ifconfig Cm readnvm
on the same interface returns
.Er EBUSY .
.Pp
After
.Cm image-apply
or
.Cm module-update Fl m Cm emp-image ,
the chip stops servicing admin-queue commands and stays in that
state until power-cycle or reboot.
This is normal: the running EMP firmware was just replaced and is
waiting to be reloaded.
.Pp
The shadow-RAM update phase
.Pq Cm image-apply Fl s , No or Cm module-update No of a shadow-RAM module
is
.Sy experimental
and not part of the supported upgrade flow.
The chip's EMP firmware regenerates most shadow-RAM modules on its own,
so a normal
.Cm image-apply
.Pq without Fl s
already produces a complete, version-consistent upgrade; this can be
confirmed with
.Cm image-diff ,
which compares each module on the chip against the image.
Direct shadow-RAM writes have proven fragile and chip-state-dependent
(intermittent
.Er EIO )
and several shadow-RAM modules are read-only or firmware-managed and
reject writes outright.
